* sg13g2-pll :: sim/sg13cmos5l-closed-loop-lock (issue #50, Part of #16)
* STANDALONE PFD-ONLY diagnostic -- Scope item 1 of issue #50: confirm or
* rule out a PFD-level polarity issue by driving the frozen `pfd` subckt
* directly with a KNOWN REF/FB phase relationship (a fixed, per-cycle
* leading offset -- not two independent free-running clocks) and reading
* `UP`/`DN` back out. This is NOT part of either closed-loop deck
* (`tb_pll_closed.sp.tmpl` / `tb_pll_proposal.sp.tmpl`) and does not touch
* `cp`, `loop_filter`, `vco`, `divider_chain` or `lock_detector` at all --
* it isolates the PFD's own combinational/sequential behaviour from
* everything downstream.
*
* `../testbench/run_pfd_diag.sh` substitutes @CORNER_MOS@ (cornerMOShv.lib
* section), @TEMP@ (degC), @VDD@ (V), @TREF@/@TREFH@ (period / high time of
* BOTH pulse trains, s -- same period, only the phase differs), @REF_DELAY@/
* @FB_DELAY@ (s -- exactly one of the two is 0, the other is the fixed
* per-cycle lead/lag under test), @PFD_INCLUDE@ (which of the two PFD
* netlists below this run instantiates), @TSTOP@, @TPRINT@, @TMAX@ and
* @TAVG0@ per that script's own two cases (`reflead`: REF's edge arrives
* first every cycle -- the PFD should read this as "FB needs to catch up";
* `fblead`: FB's edge arrives first every cycle -- the PFD should read this
* as "FB needs to slow down") crossed with its own two PFD variants
* (`asdrawn`: verbatim `../netlist-snapshots/pfd.spice`; `fixed`: a
* diagnostic-only patch to that netlist's reset-generation inverter count --
* see `run_pfd_diag.sh`'s own header for exactly what changes and why).
*
* @PFD_INCLUDE@ is one of `pfd_snap.spice` (verbatim frozen snapshot, no
* edit, no strip) or `pfd_fixed_diag.spice` (the diagnostic patch,
* NOT the committed design -- see `run_pfd_diag.sh`).

.lib $PDK_ROOT/$PDK/libs.tech/ngspice/models/cornerMOShv.lib @CORNER_MOS@
.option scale=1 temp=@TEMP@

.include @PFD_INCLUDE@

Vdd_pfd vdd_pfd 0 dc @VDD@

* Both pulse trains share one period/high-time; only the START DELAY differs
* between REF and FB, so the two edges keep the SAME lead/lag relationship
* every single cycle (a fixed phase offset, not a frequency difference) --
* this is what lets a short transient give an unambiguous steady-state
* UP/DN duty-cycle reading instead of a beat pattern.
Vref ref 0 pulse(0 @VDD@ @REF_DELAY@ 100p 100p @TREFH@ @TREF@)
Vfb  fb  0 pulse(0 @VDD@ @FB_DELAY@  100p 100p @TREFH@ @TREF@)

* .subckt pfd REF FB UP DN VDD VSS
XPFD ref fb up dn vdd_pfd 0 pfd

.tran @TPRINT@ @TSTOP@ 0 @TMAX@

.control
run
* Skip the first two reference cycles (startup/reset transient) before
* averaging -- both cases share the same @TAVG0@, set by the driver script.
meas tran up_avg avg v(up) from=@TAVG0@ to=@TSTOP@
meas tran dn_avg avg v(dn) from=@TAVG0@ to=@TSTOP@
print up_avg dn_avg
linearize v(ref) v(fb) v(up) v(dn)
wrdata wave.dat v(ref) v(fb) v(up) v(dn)
.endc
.end
